[THOUGHT] The supervisor has identified critical missing data needed to distinguish isolated gonococcal septic arthritis from disseminated gonococcal infection (DGI) — a clinically important distinction affecting management and prognosis. Specifically, I must assess for:  
- Skin lesions (petechiae, pustules) — hallmark of DGI;  
- Tenosynovitis (e.g., tendon sheath tenderness/swelling in hands/feet);  
- Systemic signs (fever, elevated CRP/ESR, blood WBC);  
- Blood cultures (to detect bacteremia);  
- Confirmatory *N. gonorrhoeae* testing (synovial fluid culture or PCR — Gram stain alone is supportive but not definitive per guidelines).  

Since synovial fluid Gram stain was positive but culture/PCR status is “Not specified”, and no systemic or dermatologic data exists, the most efficient next step is to request synovial fluid culture and PCR — as this directly confirms the etiologic pathogen and supports specificity of joint involvement. Blood cultures and skin exam are also essential, but per atomic inquiry rule, I must address one gap per turn. Synovial fluid confirmatory testing is highest yield for diagnostic precision and was explicitly flagged as insufficient.

[TEST] Synovial fluid culture and *Neisseria gonorrhoeae* nucleic acid amplification test (NAAT/PCR).

